1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:04,100
One part of your business is
quietly holding up all the rest.

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:06,740
Pull it, and the whole thing comes down.

3
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:10,300
And most founders can't actually
tell you which part it is.

4
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:13,160
And if you're still just
sitting on a new business idea,

5
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:14,900
you can't see it yet either.

6
00:00:16,070 --> 00:00:17,270
That's not carelessness.

7
00:00:17,690 --> 00:00:19,850
It's how everyone is
taught to map a business.

8
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:21,870
The Business Model Canvas,

9
00:00:22,370 --> 00:00:23,510
nine neat boxes,

10
00:00:23,870 --> 00:00:27,270
filled in like a form over an
afternoon, and called strategy.

11
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:31,050
But a real business isn't a set of boxes.

12
00:00:31,770 --> 00:00:33,350
It's an interconnected system,

13
00:00:33,590 --> 00:00:37,730
and every system has a load-bearing
part that you can't see until

14
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:38,850
the day it falls.

15
00:00:39,530 --> 00:00:41,110
Today, we find yours.

16
00:00:42,260 --> 00:00:46,400
We will also look at four parts of
the Business Model Canvas that AI has

17
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:48,060
rewritten in the last three years.

18
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:50,060
And if you stay till the end,

19
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:52,920
I will give you a free tool
that finds your weak spots.

20
00:00:54,390 --> 00:00:58,370
The Business Model Canvas is a
one-page map of how a business works.

21
00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:03,070
Alexander Osterwalder and Yves Pigneur
popularised it in their 2010 book,

22
00:01:03,490 --> 00:01:07,850
Nine Blocks, covering everything from
how you serve to how you make money.

23
00:01:09,090 --> 00:01:12,910
Most people fill in the boxes
once in a workshop and the canvas

24
00:01:12,950 --> 00:01:14,290
turns into a form.

25
00:01:14,690 --> 00:01:17,050
But those nine blocks aren't just a list,

26
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:18,570
they're wired together.

27
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:22,420
Your Key Resources feed
your Key Activities.

28
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:25,540
Your Activities produce
your Value Proposition.

29
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:29,220
The one thing everything
else exists to deliver.

30
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:32,100
In engineering,
we call that the constraint.

31
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:34,380
Everything has to flow through it.

32
00:01:35,130 --> 00:01:38,450
Your Channels carry your value
proposition to your Customer Segments.

33
00:01:39,110 --> 00:01:43,250
The money that flows back your Revenue
Streams is the feedback signal.

34
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:46,250
It's the system telling you
whether any of it worked.

35
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:49,700
It's how I was trained to read any system.

36
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:53,080
Skip the boxes, draw the architecture,

37
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:54,640
trace the flows,

38
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:59,520
find out exactly what is connected and
has direct influence on each other.

39
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:02,940
So don't just ask whether
every box is filled in.

40
00:02:03,670 --> 00:02:04,690
More importantly,

41
00:02:05,130 --> 00:02:07,210
ask where the system fails first.

42
00:02:07,890 --> 00:02:09,550
That might be your current weakness,

43
00:02:09,930 --> 00:02:12,310
but that block is actually
your leverage point.

44
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:17,310
The one that fails first and takes
the most of your system down with it.

45
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:19,980
This isn't a new idea.

46
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:21,740
It's the Pareto principle,

47
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:23,460
the eighty-twenty rule.

48
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:28,020
Roughly eighty percent of your results
come from twenty percent of your business.

49
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:29,900
In industrial engineering,

50
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:32,240
we use it to find the
bottleneck in a system.

51
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:35,240
Today, we're using that exact same logic.

52
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:39,800
It matters even more now
than it did a few years ago,

53
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:42,880
because AI has moved four
of the canvas blocks.

54
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:44,620
I'll show you which four,

55
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:47,880
and what each now makes more
possible than ever before.

56
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:52,800
But first, I'll show you two things
a system does that a form never will.

57
00:02:53,990 --> 00:02:55,650
Nothing changes in isolation.

58
00:02:56,450 --> 00:02:58,410
A great example of this is Netflix.

59
00:02:59,130 --> 00:03:02,190
They began in 1997 mailing DVDs.

60
00:03:02,490 --> 00:03:04,510
In 2007, they added streaming.

61
00:03:05,270 --> 00:03:07,430
On the canvas, that's
simply one new channel.

62
00:03:07,970 --> 00:03:10,630
However, five other blocks moved with it.

63
00:03:11,610 --> 00:03:15,370
Their Key Resources went
from discs and warehouses to

64
00:03:15,410 --> 00:03:16,810
software and bandwidth.

65
00:03:17,590 --> 00:03:21,650
Key Activities went from shipping
to licensing, then producing.

66
00:03:22,450 --> 00:03:26,510
Key Partners grew from postal services
to include internet providers.

67
00:03:27,530 --> 00:03:29,450
Costs and Revenue Followed.

68
00:03:29,830 --> 00:03:31,230
Nobody planned that cascade.

69
00:03:31,830 --> 00:03:32,950
In a wired system,

70
00:03:33,330 --> 00:03:35,050
you don't get to change just one thing.

71
00:03:35,590 --> 00:03:38,810
Try it. Pick one block in
your own business and ask,

72
00:03:39,110 --> 00:03:41,030
what else moves if it does?

73
00:03:42,650 --> 00:03:46,450
What looks like overhead on the
canvas can be one thing no competitor

74
00:03:46,490 --> 00:03:47,710
can easily copy.

75
00:03:48,470 --> 00:03:49,890
Takealot is a great example.

76
00:03:50,390 --> 00:03:53,290
It is South Africa's biggest
pure-play online retailer,

77
00:03:53,610 --> 00:03:57,650
despite Amazon entering the South
African market over two years ago.

78
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:02,480
Fill in its canvas and the operational
side reads like a liability.

79
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:05,180
Distribution centres in Cape Town,

80
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:06,480
Johannesburg and Durban.

81
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:10,580
Thousands of contracted drivers making
over a million deliveries a month.

82
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:13,440
On a spreadsheet, that's
a mountain of cost.

83
00:04:14,550 --> 00:04:16,710
But that mountain is the moat.

84
00:04:17,310 --> 00:04:19,610
A rival can copy the website in a weekend.

85
00:04:20,370 --> 00:04:22,270
Copying the warehouses, the drivers,

86
00:04:22,470 --> 00:04:23,810
the reach across nine provinces?

87
00:04:24,550 --> 00:04:27,730
That's years, and capital
most rivals don't have.

88
00:04:28,270 --> 00:04:31,590
And it's why Takealot's market
share actually grew in 2024.

89
00:04:32,130 --> 00:04:33,390
The year Amazon arrived.

90
00:04:34,550 --> 00:04:38,450
A form sorts blocks into
cheap ones and expensive ones.

91
00:04:39,090 --> 00:04:41,110
A system asks a sharper question.

92
00:04:41,770 --> 00:04:43,170
Which of these, if it vanished,

93
00:04:43,610 --> 00:04:45,070
could a competitor not rebuild?

94
00:04:45,850 --> 00:04:48,310
That block isn't a cost. It's an asset.

95
00:04:48,830 --> 00:04:51,450
And the founders who
win defend it like one.

96
00:04:52,550 --> 00:04:53,710
Hold both of those.

97
00:04:54,310 --> 00:04:55,850
Nothing changes in isolation,

98
00:04:56,230 --> 00:04:58,930
and the heaviest block can
be the one worth keeping.

99
00:04:59,710 --> 00:05:02,090
Now imagine four blocks moving at once.

100
00:05:02,590 --> 00:05:05,690
Cheaply enough that the
copyable ones turn to paper.

101
00:05:06,410 --> 00:05:08,410
That's what AI did in the last few years.

102
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:11,760
AI didn't redraw the canvas.

103
00:05:12,420 --> 00:05:16,580
It changed what four of the
blocks cost to run in headcount,

104
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:20,440
capital and time for software
and knowledge work businesses,

105
00:05:21,140 --> 00:05:23,360
doing depending serving reaching,

106
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:26,180
Key Activities, Key Partners,

107
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:29,020
Customer Relationships, Channels.

108
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:32,080
Each gets the same five moves.

109
00:05:32,780 --> 00:05:36,260
What changed? What it
makes possible? Real proof.

110
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:38,920
A fifteen-minute trial you can run today.

111
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:41,360
And where the cascade goes next.

112
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:44,260
Let's start with doing.

113
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:49,880
Key Activities. The recurring work
that turns your inputs into your offer.

114
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:53,400
Drafting, building, producing, analysing.

115
00:05:53,860 --> 00:05:56,760
It used to scale basically
one to one with people.

116
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:59,280
More work meant more hires.

117
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:02,140
That link has loosened significantly,

118
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:03,820
especially for startups.

119
00:06:05,090 --> 00:06:08,750
One person can now carry the work
that used to need a small team.

120
00:06:09,410 --> 00:06:11,650
The weekly report, the batch of quotes,

121
00:06:12,030 --> 00:06:13,890
the first draft of a product page.

122
00:06:14,550 --> 00:06:17,390
That means the cost of doing
stops being the barrier.

123
00:06:18,820 --> 00:06:22,180
Now, whether it's worth
doing becomes the question.

124
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:26,300
If you're holding an idea because it
feels like a team's worth of work,

125
00:06:26,620 --> 00:06:28,280
this is the block that moved for you.

126
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:31,640
Here's how far it can go.

127
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:36,260
One developer started an AI
app builder called Base44,

128
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:38,380
and about six months later,

129
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:39,740
in June 2025,

130
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:42,140
Wix announced it was acquiring it.

131
00:06:42,910 --> 00:06:47,690
CTech called it a one-man startup with
no outside funding, built with AI.

132
00:06:48,530 --> 00:06:50,750
At the deal, it had
fewer than ten employees.

133
00:06:51,590 --> 00:06:53,010
He was an experienced founder,

134
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:56,530
so he's an outlier, not a promise.
But look at what moved.

135
00:06:57,130 --> 00:06:59,070
Headcount, capital and time.

136
00:07:00,450 --> 00:07:01,890
Now your fifteen minutes.

137
00:07:02,670 --> 00:07:04,910
Pick the one task you repeat every week.

138
00:07:05,270 --> 00:07:06,570
Write it down as steps.

139
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:11,250
Then give Claude or your favourite
AI the steps and one real input.

140
00:07:11,950 --> 00:07:14,750
Compare the results and
the time with your own.

141
00:07:15,370 --> 00:07:18,970
Do this with multiple tasks and
it compounds into real value.

142
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:23,260
Keep a human review step,
at least for now.

143
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:26,960
Where the cascade goes
next, Cost Structure.

144
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:29,380
Salaries are fixed costs.

145
00:07:30,020 --> 00:07:31,320
Subscriptions are variable.

146
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:33,660
You can scale them up or down monthly.

147
00:07:34,540 --> 00:07:37,060
Doing shifts from one to the other.

148
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:40,020
Next, Depending,

149
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:42,400
Key Partners.

150
00:07:43,320 --> 00:07:45,920
The agencies, contractors and freelancers,

151
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:48,000
you pay to do what you can't do yourself.

152
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:50,780
Hiring them used to be the only move.

153
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:53,940
Now there's a second, Rent an AI Model.

154
00:07:55,130 --> 00:07:58,750
First drafts and agency grade
routine work can now happen

155
00:07:58,790 --> 00:08:01,070
in-house with no retainer.

156
00:08:01,570 --> 00:08:04,110
And that actually puts a
new name on your canvas,

157
00:08:04,570 --> 00:08:06,450
the AI model provider itself.

158
00:08:07,190 --> 00:08:08,510
That's a rented dependency,

159
00:08:09,130 --> 00:08:10,450
a single point of failure.

160
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:15,540
In early 2024, Duolingo let
about ten percent of its

161
00:08:15,580 --> 00:08:19,180
contractors go and said part of
that could be attributed to AI.

162
00:08:20,370 --> 00:08:22,610
Then, in April 2025,

163
00:08:23,230 --> 00:08:27,270
its CEO told staff the company would
gradually stop using contractors for

164
00:08:27,310 --> 00:08:28,630
work AI can handle.

165
00:08:29,290 --> 00:08:32,370
Backlash followed. By May, he clarified,

166
00:08:32,970 --> 00:08:34,950
he hadn't meant that hiring would stop,

167
00:08:35,370 --> 00:08:37,210
the company would keep hiring people.

168
00:08:38,130 --> 00:08:39,630
So the swap is underway,

169
00:08:40,250 --> 00:08:41,630
and how you make it matters.

170
00:08:42,430 --> 00:08:44,270
Who does the work is a wiring decision.

171
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:46,430
Architecture determines scale.

172
00:08:47,350 --> 00:08:49,370
Think of Airbnb's hosts,

173
00:08:50,090 --> 00:08:51,290
Duolingo's contractors,

174
00:08:51,910 --> 00:08:55,470
your AI stack. It's the same
block, but different owners.

175
00:08:56,770 --> 00:08:58,370
Now take fifteen minutes.

176
00:08:59,150 --> 00:09:02,970
List every contractor,
agency and paid tool you use.

177
00:09:03,510 --> 00:09:05,690
Write the one deliverable
you buy from each.

178
00:09:06,330 --> 00:09:08,490
Ask Claude for a first draft of it.

179
00:09:09,090 --> 00:09:12,930
Then keep only the ones where you'd
accept the draft with light edits.

180
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:16,890
And add the AI model vendor
to your Key Partners.

181
00:09:17,730 --> 00:09:20,510
Where the cascade goes
next, Key Resources,

182
00:09:21,230 --> 00:09:24,610
because your prompts and
workflows become an asset you own.

183
00:09:25,210 --> 00:09:27,730
And concentration risk
moves to the vendor.

184
00:09:29,260 --> 00:09:30,780
Then, Serving.

185
00:09:31,780 --> 00:09:32,960
Customer Relationships.

186
00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:37,380
How You Answer, Help and
Keep the People You Sell To.

187
00:09:38,140 --> 00:09:42,340
First Responses and Routine Support
No Longer Need a Person on Shift.

188
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:46,960
The Block stops scaling with
Headcount and starts working in tiers.

189
00:09:48,210 --> 00:09:51,030
A small team can now
be always on and quick,

190
00:09:51,390 --> 00:09:52,670
even at two in the morning,

191
00:09:53,190 --> 00:09:55,570
and rather spend its
people on judgment calls.

192
00:09:56,230 --> 00:09:58,210
The work becomes designing the handoff,

193
00:09:58,890 --> 00:10:00,570
what the AI assistant handles,

194
00:10:00,970 --> 00:10:02,530
and when a person steps in.

195
00:10:03,270 --> 00:10:04,690
In early 2024,

196
00:10:05,090 --> 00:10:09,190
Klarna reported that its AI assistant
handled 2.3 million conversations

197
00:10:09,230 --> 00:10:10,530
in its first month,

198
00:10:10,910 --> 00:10:12,850
two-thirds of its customer chats.

199
00:10:13,470 --> 00:10:16,590
They said that was work equal
to 700 full-time agents.

200
00:10:17,470 --> 00:10:21,250
It also reported resolution in
under two minutes instead of eleven.

201
00:10:21,890 --> 00:10:23,850
Those numbers come from Klarna itself,

202
00:10:24,250 --> 00:10:25,570
not an independent audit.

203
00:10:26,530 --> 00:10:28,470
Then, in May 2025,

204
00:10:29,050 --> 00:10:33,570
Klarna's CEO said cost had been too big
a factor in how it organised support

205
00:10:33,930 --> 00:10:35,390
and quality had suffered.

206
00:10:35,970 --> 00:10:37,930
They started hiring human agents again,

207
00:10:38,250 --> 00:10:40,470
so customers could always reach a person.

208
00:10:41,330 --> 00:10:44,430
The Assistant still handled
two-thirds of inquiries.

209
00:10:45,130 --> 00:10:46,630
So here's the handoff redesigned.

210
00:10:47,030 --> 00:10:48,750
AI carries the routine tier,

211
00:10:49,210 --> 00:10:50,990
while people keep the judgment tier.

212
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:53,520
Now, your fifteen minutes.

213
00:10:54,140 --> 00:10:56,880
Paste your last few customer
messages into Claude.

214
00:10:57,280 --> 00:10:59,960
Ask it to sort them into
routine and judgment,

215
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:02,520
and draft replies to the routine ones.

216
00:11:03,590 --> 00:11:06,490
Then, decide the handoff
rule for the rest.

217
00:11:07,370 --> 00:11:09,050
Where the cascade goes next.

218
00:11:09,510 --> 00:11:10,710
Cost Structure again.

219
00:11:11,270 --> 00:11:13,030
Because support turns variable.

220
00:11:13,750 --> 00:11:14,790
And Revenue Streams?

221
00:11:15,310 --> 00:11:18,490
Because keeping customers depends
on where that handoff sits.

222
00:11:19,130 --> 00:11:20,850
Customer experience matters.

223
00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:24,160
Last, Reaching.

224
00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:27,740
Channels. How customers find you.

225
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:30,320
What changed is where they start.

226
00:11:31,020 --> 00:11:34,720
AI assistants have become a
place many customers begin.

227
00:11:35,420 --> 00:11:38,740
They describe what they need
and the assistant goes looking.

228
00:11:39,740 --> 00:11:43,740
Ask it for a waterproof day pack
that fits a laptop and a lunchbox.

229
00:11:44,380 --> 00:11:45,800
It isn't matching keywords,

230
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:47,780
it's matching a need.

231
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:52,920
And a small maker of exactly that
pack now has a way to be found.

232
00:11:53,680 --> 00:11:56,280
On its earnings call in August 2026,

233
00:11:56,940 --> 00:12:00,840
Shopify said AI-driven traffic
and orders to its stores tripled

234
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:01,960
from a year earlier.

235
00:12:02,780 --> 00:12:04,940
Shopify didn't share absolute numbers,

236
00:12:05,080 --> 00:12:07,040
so we can't see how big that base is.

237
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:11,480
It also said AI complements
search rather than replacing it.

238
00:12:12,420 --> 00:12:16,420
And 75% of AI attributed purchases came

239
00:12:16,460 --> 00:12:19,220
from outside its top
hundred product categories.

240
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,600
Shopify's president said that has
especially helped the long tail,

241
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:26,240
meaning the many small niche sellers.

242
00:12:26,860 --> 00:12:28,160
He also explained why.

243
00:12:28,780 --> 00:12:31,860
Normal search favours what's
popular for a few keywords.

244
00:12:32,950 --> 00:12:36,890
Whereas AI agents match a
buyer's specific intent using

245
00:12:36,930 --> 00:12:38,130
richer product data.

246
00:12:38,650 --> 00:12:43,410
So what it takes to be found is shifting
from popularity towards specificity.

247
00:12:44,070 --> 00:12:48,030
The catch is that an AI assistant can
only recommend what it can clearly read.

248
00:12:49,220 --> 00:12:50,860
Your fifteen minutes.

249
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:55,580
Type the exact question your customers
would ask into an AI assistant.

250
00:12:56,320 --> 00:12:59,380
Note whether you appear
and what it says about you.

251
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:02,920
Then make your page answer that question.

252
00:13:03,700 --> 00:13:05,220
Specifics, sizes,

253
00:13:05,700 --> 00:13:07,640
what it costs, who it's for,

254
00:13:08,440 --> 00:13:10,140
where the cascade goes next.

255
00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:15,640
